export default {"id":"dsp-level-11","number":11,"title":"Reverberation","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"1. Dattorro (JAES 1997): reverberation creates an ambient space; the presented network is a small recursive design with knobs for decay, diffusion (decorrelation), high-frequency damping, input bandwidth, predelay 2. History Dattorro names:","modules":[{"id":"dsp-m-12-1","code":"12","title":"overview dsp part 12","blurb":"overview dsp part 12","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"dsp-part-12-overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"LEVEL 11 — Reverberation overview","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Objective:</strong> Describe algorithmic reverb as delay networks with diffusion, and convolution reverb as FIR against a measured <code>h</code>."}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"dsp-m-11-1-2","code":"11.1","title":"— What a reverberator must do","blurb":"1. Dattorro (JAES 1997): reverberation creates an ambient space; the presented network is a small recursive design with knobs for decay, diffusion (decorrelation), high-frequency damping, input bandwidth, predelay 2. History Dattorro names:","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"dsp-part-12-1","title":"What a reverberator must do","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Module 11.1 — What a reverberator must do","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Outcomes:</strong> Name echo density, decay, damping, and predelay as measurable goals."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Topic coverage:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["Dattorro (JAES 1997): reverberation creates an ambient space; the presented network is a small recursive design with knobs for decay, diffusion (decorrelation), high-frequency damping, input bandwidth, predelay","History Dattorro names: Schroeder delay-line work (Bell Labs, early 1960s); Moorer; Gardner’s chronicle; Griesinger / Blesser commercial networks","Eigen-tone density: Schroeder’s criterion of about 3 eigen-tones per hertz is cited; Dattorro notes it is not a hard rule and that his efficient network still sounds good with far less memory","Plate class: musicians often want instant high-density reflections and exponential decay, not a literal room (Dattorro §1.2)","Live <strong>Reverb</strong> (Standard) is the laboratory algorithmic device. Teach the <em>manual’s</em> parameter names. Do not claim Live Reverb <em>is</em> the Dattorro topology"],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>mustCover:</strong> Predelay, decay, damping, diffusion as Dattorro’s knobs; Live Reverb as the Standard device; no topology impersonation   <strong>mustNotCover:</strong> Hybrid Reverb (Suite) as required   <strong>Primary sources:</strong> Dattorro §§0–1.3 and Table 1; Live ch. 28 Reverb   <strong>Practice:</strong> Same clap through Live Reverb; vary Decay and HF damping; write what changed in the tail   <strong>Checkpoint:</strong> Learner defines diffusion vs decay vs damping without synonyms-as-definitions"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"dsp-m-11-2-3","code":"11.2","title":"— Diffusers, tanks, allpass lattices","blurb":"1. Four input diffusers (allpass lattices) decorrelate the input before the tank. Zero diffusion = zero allpass coefficients; values near 1 buzz; Dattorro prefers magnitudes nearer |0.5| 2. Tank: four recirculating diffusers in a figure-eig","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"dsp-part-12-2","title":"Diffusers, tanks, allpass lattices","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Module 11.2 — Diffusers, tanks, allpass lattices","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Outcomes:</strong> Read Dattorro fig. 1 at block level."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Topic coverage:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["Four <strong>input diffusers</strong> (allpass lattices) decorrelate the input before the tank. Zero diffusion = zero allpass coefficients; values near 1 buzz; Dattorro prefers magnitudes nearer <code>|0.5|</code>","<strong>Tank:</strong> four recirculating diffusers in a figure-eight. Decay coefficients near 1 hold sound indefinitely; damping filter removes high frequencies each lap","Output is a large set of taps (not shown in fig. 1; see Dattorro Table 2)","Delay-line taps at nodes 24 and 48 are modulated (chorus-like inside the tank)","Table 1 default <code>Fs = 29761</code> Hz is <strong>Dattorro’s example rate</strong>, not this studio’s project rate. Do not copy those millisecond values into a 44.1 kHz Live set without rescaling, and do not require students to rebuild fig. 1"],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>mustCover:</strong> Input diffuser vs tank; allpass as decorrelator; Table 1 <code>Fs</code> is his example   <strong>mustNotCover:</strong> Assigned implementation of fig. 1; Hybrid Reverb convolution IR management   <strong>Primary sources:</strong> Dattorro fig. 1, §1.3, Table 1; Rocchesso §3.6.2   <strong>Practice:</strong> On Live Reverb, identify which <em>Live</em> knobs correspond <em>functionally</em> (not structurally) to decay, damping, predelay, and diffusion   <strong>Checkpoint:</strong> Learner explains why input diffusion exists (avoid hearing the loop)"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"dsp-m-11-3-4","code":"11.3","title":"— Convolution reverb as FIR","blurb":"1. Level 4: any LTI room is h. Convolution reverb is y = h * x with h recorded in a space (or modeled) 2. Length: a 2 s IR at 44.1 kHz is 2 × 44100 = 88200 taps — arithmetic that explains the CPU cost vs a recirculating IIR tank","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"dsp-part-12-3","title":"Convolution reverb as FIR","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Module 11.3 — Convolution reverb as FIR","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Outcomes:</strong> State that convolving with a measured room impulse response is an FIR filter."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Topic coverage:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["Level 4: any LTI room is <code>h</code>. Convolution reverb is <code>y = h * x</code> with <code>h</code> recorded in a space (or modeled)","Length: a 2 s IR at 44.1 kHz is <code>2 × 44100 = 88200</code> taps — arithmetic that explains the CPU cost vs a recirculating IIR tank","This studio does <strong>not</strong> include Hybrid Reverb. Convolution is taught as a concept and, if needed, as an offline bounce of an IR through Live’s <strong>external</strong> routing — only if the lesson opens a sourced procedure. Otherwise the concept is assessed on paper plus the FIR cost calculation","Fact Sheet freeze: frozen Arrangement tails are rendered audio and stop dead; unfrozen algorithmic tails continue. Convolution vs algorithmic is a good place to restate that"],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>mustCover:</strong> Convolution reverb = FIR with a measured <code>h</code>; tap-count cost example; no Hybrid Reverb requirement   <strong>mustNotCover:</strong> Shipping a copyrighted IR library as course material   <strong>Primary sources:</strong> Rocchesso §1.4 and §3.6; Dattorro contrast with convolution-scale memory; Fact Sheet freeze tails   <strong>Practice:</strong> Compute tap count for 1 s and 3 s IRs at 44.1 kHz and 48 kHz   <strong>Checkpoint:</strong> Learner states why a recirculating tank can be cheaper than a long FIR"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Gate I — Reverb literacy","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Self-assess: name Dattorro’s four knob jobs; contrast tank-plus-diffusers with FIR convolution; keep Live Reverb’s unknown internals unlabeled."}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
